Show the pad's controls, on the help screen that already exists.

A console port has a problem a desktop build does not: nothing on screen says what the
buttons do. Upstream's help screen lists thirteen key names, which is the right page for a
desktop and the wrong one here - there is no keyboard in the room, and the one thing a
player cannot work out for themselves is which button charges a jump.

**The screen already existed**, which is why this is a patch to one function rather than a
new game mode, a new entry in `TGameMode` and a new set of mode functions. `HELP` is in
upstream's enum and reachable from the menus; only what it draws changes.

The wireframe is the shared one from `common/assets/controls`, loaded straight to a GL id
with `CTexture::LoadTexture` rather than added to `data/textures/textures.lst` - the
picture is ours and shared with every title here, so it ships in the *packaged* data
(`make package` copies it) and upstream's own data stays as upstream ships it. A load
failure draws the list without the picture rather than refusing to open the screen.

The strings are English here rather than `Trans.Text` indices. Upstream's 44-56 *are* the
key list, so reusing them would print keyboard help beside a picture of a pad, and adding
new indices means editing thirteen translation files of somebody else's data for a screen
that exists because this platform has no keyboard. It is the same trade the existing
"Press any key to return to the main menu" line already makes.

